@* Section I — Professionalism *@
<p>
    This section evaluates your professional aptitude — effective communication, ethical behaviour, and
    adherence to professional standards. It asks whether you can conduct yourself as a professional and
    carry the responsibilities that come with the designation. It is worth roughly <strong>10% of the
    exam (7–10 questions)</strong>.
</p>

<h2 id="definition"><span class="rref">I.1</span> Definition &amp; Interpretation of Professional Status</h2>
<p>
    A professional possesses specialized knowledge, rigorous training, and education in particular skills
    and techniques, together with a comprehensive understanding of the scientific, historical, or scholarly
    principles that underpin them. Professionals are held to strict standards of achievement and behaviour —
    through formal systems or shared belief — and are obligated to pursue continuous learning and to engage
    in work that is primarily focused on serving the public.
</p>

<h2 id="roles"><span class="rref">I.2</span> Roles &amp; Responsibilities of Professionals in Society</h2>
<p>
    When considering how professionals engage with society, six elements must be kept in mind:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>The delicate nature of <strong>public trust</strong> and the need to preserve it</li>
    <li>Avoiding an <strong>elitist</strong> perspective</li>
    <li>Avoiding a <strong>monopoly</strong> perspective</li>
    <li>Meeting the demands of the public</li>
    <li>The increase in complexity and specialization</li>
    <li>Taking responsibility for your actions and decisions</li>
</ul>

<h3>Self-Regulation</h3>
<p>
    Each province and territory has passed an <strong>Act</strong> to establish a professional Association,
    which oversees the practice of engineering and geoscience on behalf of the provincial or territorial
    government. Membership grants the profession the authority to elect engineers and geoscientists to a
    governing council — this is what makes the profession <strong>self-regulated</strong>. Other countries,
    such as the USA, rely on government to regulate the profession instead. The council is made up mostly of
    Association members, with a few members drawn from the public.
</p>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">Why self-regulation exists</span>
    <p>
        It protects the public by allowing only qualified individuals to use titles such as
        <strong>“P.Eng.”</strong> or <strong>“Professional Geoscientist,”</strong> and by clearly defining
        the scope of practice so unqualified people cannot practise. Skilled professionals on the council are
        essential to administering the Act, and the public benefits because the Association is funded mainly
        by licensing fees rather than by provincial or federal money.
    </p>
</div>

<h2 id="professions"><span class="rref">I.3</span> Professions in Canada — Definitions &amp; Scopes of Practice</h2>
<p>
    Organizations such as Engineers Canada and your Association each offer their own definition of
    “professional engineering.” While the wording differs, they converge on shared principles: the use of
    mathematics and sciences, prioritizing <strong>public safety</strong>, applying sound engineering
    judgment, and implementing an appropriate <strong>factor of safety</strong>.
</p>

<h2 id="value"><span class="rref">I.4</span> The Value of the Professions to Society</h2>
<p>
    This part of the syllabus is short and straightforward: the public’s best interests are safeguarded by
    entrusting only skilled and licensed professionals to oversee the quality of professional services.
</p>
